Subject: [PATCH 11178/11362] [outer-loop] Correction: the orphaned-test defect
 is not live; I sampled mid-task

At 06:20Z I recorded a fourth orphaned test in the current milestone, called
the defect live rather than historical, put a rate on it of roughly four in
five days, and filed an operator decision asking for a pre-commit check.

My own follow-up refutes all of it. The exp7013 module exists, in both
python/carnot and scripts/experiments, and re-measuring gives 3 orphan-import
tests rather than 4. I had measured inside the window between the test file
landing and the module landing, so the fourth was a transient that resolved
minutes later. exp7013 then completed with a clean artifact: an honest null,
602 seconds of real live_llm_inference, 11 gate checks matching by sha256.

This is the standing base-rate instruction violated by me in the same session
that keeps quoting it. One mid-task sample became a rate and then a
recommendation.

Operator decision 12 is withdrawn and would have been harmful. A commit-time
check refusing a test that imports a missing module fires on the normal
mid-task state of every conductor experiment, blocking legitimate work on a
condition that resolves itself. That is the check-that-cries-wolf CLAUDE.md
warns costs more than the gap it closes.

What survives: the three files from 2026-08-31 are genuinely orphaned, their
modules were never committed at all, and they have been un-collectable for five
days. Only the recurrence claim is retracted. The right detector for the real
class is a periodic sweep taken when no task is running, which can tell a
five-day-old orphan from a five-minute-old one.

